UPDATE public.stories 
SET content = 'The great Persian poet Rumi offered one of the most profound teachings about the human experience in his poem about the guest house. He wrote:

"This being human is a guest house. Every morning brings a new arrival—a joy, a depression, a meanness, some momentary awareness that comes as an unexpected visitor. Welcome and entertain them all! Even if they arrive as a crowd of sorrows, violently sweeping your house empty of its furniture. Still, treat each guest honorably. They may be clearing you out for some new delight."

He continued: "The dark thought, the shame, the malice—meet them at the door laughing, and invite them in. Be grateful for whoever comes, because each has been sent as a guide from beyond, each teaches you something you need to learn."

This teaching challenges our deepest instincts. We spend so much energy trying to curate our inner experience, to invite only certain feelings and exile others. We want joy but not sadness, confidence but not fear, peace but not anger. We treat our difficult emotions like intruders to be expelled rather than guests to be welcomed.

But Rumi understood something profound: every emotion carries wisdom if we have the courage to listen. Depression might be teaching us we are living out of alignment with our values. Anger might be showing us where our boundaries have been violated. Fear might be protecting us or showing us what matters most. Even shame, as painful as it is, might be revealing where we have acted against our own integrity.

When we resist our emotions, we suffer doubly—first from the emotion itself, and second from our resistance to it. When we welcome them as Rumi suggests, we transform our relationship to our inner experience. The emotion may still be difficult, but we are no longer at war with ourselves.'
WHERE title = 'The Guest House';

UPDATE public.stories 
SET content = 'Rumi told a story about a group of people who encountered an elephant for the first time, but they found it in complete darkness. With no light to see by, they could only touch the elephant and try to understand what it was through their sense of touch alone.

One person touched the elephant''s trunk and declared: "This elephant is like a large snake or a thick rope—long, flexible, and powerful!" Another person felt the elephant''s ear and said: "You are completely wrong! This elephant is like a great fan, thin and flat and moving back and forth." A third person wrapped their arms around the elephant''s leg and insisted: "You are both mistaken! This elephant is like a massive pillar or tree trunk, round and solid and tall."

A fourth person ran their hand along the elephant''s side and said: "None of you understand! This creature is like a vast wall, broad and rough and seemingly endless." Each person, having touched only one part of the elephant, was convinced they alone understood its true nature. Each argued passionately that their perception was correct and all others were wrong.

But of course, each person was perceiving only a fragment of the whole truth. The trunk is snake-like AND the ear is fan-like AND the leg is pillar-like AND the side is wall-like. Each person was right about what they experienced, but completely wrong in thinking their experience captured the whole elephant.

Rumi''s point was profound: This is how we approach truth. We each encounter reality from our limited perspective—shaped by our experience, our culture, our moment in history, our personal wounds and gifts—and we mistake our partial view for the complete picture. The mystic touches one aspect of the Divine and calls it mercy. The philosopher touches another aspect and calls it logic. The scientist touches yet another and calls it natural law. Each is right about what they have perceived, but wrong to claim that is all there is.

True wisdom requires humility: recognizing that my view is necessarily partial, and that engaging with other perspectives does not threaten truth but helps reveal it more fully.'
WHERE title = 'The Elephant in the Dark';

UPDATE public.stories 
SET content = 'One night, people found Nasruddin, the legendary wise fool of Sufi tradition, on his hands and knees under a streetlamp, clearly searching for something. "Nasruddin, what have you lost?" they asked. "My keys," he replied, continuing to search intently in the circle of light cast by the lamp.

More people gathered to help him look, and together they searched every inch of ground under the streetlamp. After a long time with no success, someone finally asked: "Nasruddin, are you certain you lost your keys here under this streetlamp?" 

Nasruddin looked up and said: "Oh no, I did not lose them here. I lost them in my house." The people were stunned. "Then why are you searching for them here?" they asked in confusion.

Nasruddin replied simply: "Because the light is much better here!"

The crowd groaned at the absurdity, but then a few began to understand the deeper teaching. Nasruddin was showing them something about human nature that we all do but rarely admit: we look for answers where it is comfortable and well-lit, rather than where the actual problem lies.

We search for the source of our unhappiness in other people''s behavior rather than examining our own reactions. We look for meaning in status and possessions because those are easy to pursue, rather than in the harder work of examining our values and character. We seek solutions to our problems in quick fixes and surface changes because it is easier than doing the difficult work of real transformation. We read self-help books rather than sitting with our pain. We blame our circumstances rather than examining our choices.

Like Nasruddin under the streetlamp, we search where the light is good, where we feel comfortable, where the effort is minimal—even when we know, deep down, that what we seek lies in the dark, uncomfortable places we are avoiding. The teaching is clear: if you want to find what you have lost, you must be willing to search in the dark, difficult places where you actually lost it.'
WHERE title = 'Nasruddin''s Keys';